okay today I'm going to talk to you  about filtration and crystallization so  these are two different processes we're  going to see the equipment that we need  for each when we'd use each technique  and how they operate so let's start with  filtration so filtration is when you  want to separate an insoluble solid from  a liquid so what insoluble means means  it does not dissolve so let's have a  look at an example so here I have a  beaker and in that beaker I have a  mixture of sand and water  so my sand is my in soluble solid  because sand does not dissolve in water  so to be able to separate the water from  the sand I'm going to use filtration  which is a really simple process all  that I need is a beaker a funnel and in  the funnel goes some filter paper so  filter paper has these tiny little pores  and that allows certain things to flow  through but larger insoluble things it  does not allow to flow through so all  you need to do is get your mixture of  your insoluble solid and liquid and you  put it through there now all of the  insoluble solid is going to stay in the  filter paper because it's too big to fit  through whereas the liquid will drip  through and collect at the bottom so  you've beautifully managed to separate  the insoluble solid from the liquid now  this only works for in soluble solids if  we have a soluble stuff solid we have to  use a different process called  crystallization crystallization yeah so  if let's say this was salty water  and sand the salty water because the  salt is dissolved in the water it would  go straight through the filter paper  because it's broken up and dissolved in  solution so instead we need to do a  process called crystallization so  crystallization is how you separate a  soluble solid from a solution so a  really common example that we could look  at would just be if I had a beaker  containing salty water so sodium  chloride dissolved in water salty water  my salt is my substance which is soluble  meaning it dissolves right so this time  instead of using a funnel what you need  to do is you need to use the following  equipment so right here I have my Bunsen  burner that's what's going to provide  the heat because we need to evaporate  that water here we just have a tripod  and on the tripod will have a gauze and  on there we have an evaporating dish you  may also have heard this being called an  evaporating basin before as well so what  you need to do is you need to get that  salty water or whatever your soluble  solid is you need to get that and you  put it into your evaporating Basin and  you start to heat it what will happen is  the water will evaporate and it will  leave crystals of our salt behind that's  why it's called crystallization because  lit behind will be left loads of salt  crystals so finally once we've done that  if you want to dry it even further you  could use a bit of equipment called a  desiccator which is specially designed  to remove even more of the water to  leave us some nice crystals of salt now  we've separated it  so quick recap filtration is used to  separate an insoluble solid from liquid  all you need to do is put it through a  filter paper in a funnel the insoluble  solid that gets left behind and the  liquid goes through the crystallization  is to separate a soluble solid from a  solution that means it can dissolve all  you need to do is put it in a vaporators  dish heat it up the water evaporates and  leaves crystals of your substance behind  for further drying you put it in a  desiccator thank you for listening
 

